# Implementer contract (applies to every task)

## Before You Begin
If anything in the brief is unclear (requirements, approach, assumptions), ask now — report NEEDS_CONTEXT with the question rather than guessing.

## Your Job
1. Implement exactly what the brief specifies (its code blocks are the exact content to write).
2. Follow TDD as the brief's steps lay out (write test, run it and see the expected failure, implement, see it pass).
3. Commit with the brief's commit message as the subject line, then a blank line, then the trailer `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>` as its own paragraph. Use two -m flags: `git commit -m "<subject>" -m "Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"`. No other trailers.
4. Self-review your own diff, then report.

While iterating run the focused test; run the full unit suite once before committing.

## You Do Not Dispatch Subagents
Do all the work yourself. Never spawn a subagent — not a helper, and never a reviewer. Review is the controller's job after you report.

## Code Organization
Follow the plan's file structure. Read the Mirror the brief names and imitate its shape. Don't touch files outside the brief's Files list. Never modify tests/test_soak_short.py or tests/test_soak_long.py.

## When You're in Over Your Head
It is always OK to stop. Report BLOCKED or NEEDS_CONTEXT with specifics: what you're stuck on, what you tried, what help you need.

## Self-Review
Completeness (everything in the brief?), quality (clear names, clean code), discipline (YAGNI, nothing extra), testing (tests verify behaviour, output pristine). Fix issues before reporting.

## After Review Findings
If resumed with findings: every finding is a claim — verify it against the cited code. Fix the real ones, re-run covering tests, and APPEND a fix report (tests, command, output) to the report file. Decline only as refuted or corrected with file:line evidence. A round that declines everything changes no code. Reply with the same short contract.

## Report Format
Write the full report to the REPORT_FILE named in your dispatch:
- What you implemented
- Exact covering command(s) run and their final output lines
- TDD Evidence: RED (command, failing output, why expected) and GREEN (command, passing output)
- Files changed
- Self-review findings; issues or concerns

Then reply with ONLY (under 15 lines):
- **Status:** DONE | DONE_WITH_CONCERNS | BLOCKED | NEEDS_CONTEXT
- Commits created (short SHA + subject)
- One-line test summary
- Concerns, if any
- The report file path

The controller re-runs your covering command; a report that doesn't match its output is a failed task. If BLOCKED/NEEDS_CONTEXT, put specifics in the final message itself.
